  "tasks they care about" implies tasks that someone [3melse[0m can change — both require a shared store and a notion of
  identity. Building browser-only first would mean rewriting the task layer before notifications could do what you asked
  for.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · Does the task layer need a server, or is this a single-user browser app? → Server + shared store (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Next: the stack. This one determines tooling, test infrastructure, and deploy, and it's awkward to change once there's
  a schema and an auth flow in place.

  The relevant difference isn't language preference — it's how much of the notification layer the platform gives you for
  free. Notifications need a persistent store, background delivery (something that runs when no request is in flight),
  and eventually a push channel.

  - [1mNode + TypeScript (Express/Fastify + Postgres)[0m — boring, well-trodden, and the ecosystem for job queues, email, and
    web-push is mature. Shares a language with the existing HTML front end.
  - [1mPython (FastAPI + Postgres)[0m — equally solid; better if you or your team live in Python. Matches the micromamba/uv
    toolchain in your global config.
  - [1mA batteries-included framework (Rails / Django)[0m — auth, admin, migrations, and a job runner out of the box, which is
    a real head start on sub-projects 2 and 3. Costs more framework to learn and carry if you don't already use it.

  I'd recommend [1mNode + TypeScript[0m unless you have a standing preference, purely because the front end is already HTML/JS
  and one language is one toolchain. But your CLAUDE.md documents a serious Python setup and no Node conventions, so if
  this is going to live alongside your other work, Python is the better fit — tell me which world this app belongs to.
